%[text] # Week 1: From a Physics Model to a Computational Experiment
%[text] This Live Script follows one complete reasoning chain: physical model $\rightarrow$ scale and units $\rightarrow$ discretisation $\rightarrow$ algorithm $\rightarrow$ code $\rightarrow$ error $\rightarrow$ validation $\rightarrow$ physical interpretation.
%[text] **Physical question.** How many radioactive nuclei remain after four half-lives, and how can we decide whether a time-stepping calculation deserves our trust?
%[text] **Learning outcome.** By the end, you should be able to turn a decay law into a computational experiment, explain the Forward Euler update, and use convergence and physical checks as evidence.
%%
%[text] ## Prediction Before Computing
%[text] Before running any code, record a prediction:
%[text] 1. What fraction remains after one half-life?
%[text] 2. What fraction remains after four half-lives?
%[text] 3. Can the physical population become negative?
%[text] 4. Should a smaller timestep make Forward Euler agree more closely with the analytical solution? \
predictionRecord = "TODO: record the prediction and reasoning before running the model" %[output:4693dcf1]
%%
%[text] ## Model, Assumptions, and Parameters
%[text] The decay model assumes independent nuclei and a constant decay probability per unit time. The governing equation and analytical reference are
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\frac{dN}{dt}=-\\lambda N, \\qquad N(t)=N\_0 e^{-\\lambda t}, \\qquad \\lambda=\\frac{\\ln 2}{T\_{1/2}}. $
%[text] The parameter names make their units visible. The product $\\lambda\\Delta t$ must be dimensionless.
N0 = 1000;                        % initial population, nuclei
T_half_h = 6.0;                   % half-life, h
tMax_h = 24.0;                    % final time, h
dt_h = 0.5;                       % Forward Euler timestep, h
lambda_per_h = log(2)/T_half_h;   % decay constant, 1/h
scaleRecord = table(N0,T_half_h,tMax_h,dt_h,lambda_per_h,lambda_per_h*dt_h, ... %[output:group:0df3e699] %[output:97e3bcd2]
    'VariableNames',{'N0','halfLife_h','finalTime_h','timestep_h','lambda_per_h','lambdaTimesDt'}) %[output:group:0df3e699] %[output:97e3bcd2]
%[text] **Checkpoint.** Explain why $\\lambda\\Delta t$ has no units and why its size matters to a numerical update.
scaleExplanation = "TODO: explain the dimensionless timestep" %[output:8a3ff019]
%%
%[text] ## Analytical Reference and Limiting Cases
%[text] Evaluate the analytical model on the computational time grid. Check the initial value and the known fractions after one and four half-lives.
t_h = 0:dt_h:tMax_h;
N_exact = N0.*exp(-lambda_per_h.*t_h);
referenceRecord = table(N_exact(1)/N0,exp(-lambda_per_h*T_half_h),N_exact(end)/N0, ... %[output:group:05369e3c] %[output:4d4182a6]
    'VariableNames',{'initialFraction','oneHalfLifeFraction','fourHalfLifeFraction'}) %[output:group:05369e3c] %[output:4d4182a6]
plot(t_h,N_exact/N0,'k-','LineWidth',1.8) %[output:415608c0]
grid on %[output:415608c0]
xlabel('Time, t (h)') %[output:415608c0]
ylabel('Fraction remaining, N/N_0') %[output:415608c0]
title('Analytical radioactive-decay reference') %[output:415608c0]
%[text] **Interpretation.** Connect the initial value, decreasing trend, and long-time limit to the physical model.
referenceInterpretation = "TODO: interpret the analytical curve physically" %[output:45fba9ff]
%%
%[text] ## Discretisation and Algorithm
%[text] Forward Euler replaces the continuous derivative by a step-to-step change:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\frac{N\_{n+1}-N\_n}{\\Delta t}\\approx-\\lambda N\_n \\quad\\Rightarrow\\quad N\_{n+1}=N\_n\\left(1-\\lambda\\Delta t\right). $
%[text] Pseudocode: create the time grid; allocate storage; set the initial value; repeatedly compute the next value from the current value; then compare with reference evidence.
N_euler = nan(size(t_h));
N_euler(1) = N0;
for n = 1:numel(t_h)-1
    N_euler(n+1) = N_euler(n)*(1-lambda_per_h*dt_h);
end
finalRelativeError = abs(N_euler(end)-N_exact(end))/N_exact(end);
singleRunRecord = table(dt_h,N_euler(end)/N0,N_exact(end)/N0,100*finalRelativeError, ... %[output:group:9dfcdaab] %[output:01db9664]
    'VariableNames',{'dt_h','EulerFinalFraction','ReferenceFinalFraction','RelativeErrorPercent'}) %[output:group:9dfcdaab] %[output:01db9664]
plot(t_h,N_euler/N0,'o-',t_h,N_exact/N0,'k-','LineWidth',1.4) %[output:81cd42c8]
grid on %[output:81cd42c8]
xlabel('Time, t (h)') %[output:81cd42c8]
ylabel('Fraction remaining, N/N_0') %[output:81cd42c8]
legend('Forward Euler','Analytical reference','Location','northeast') %[output:81cd42c8]
title('Numerical result compared with a reference') %[output:81cd42c8]
%[text] **Checkpoint.** Identify the current value, update factor, and next value in the loop. Explain why syntactically correct code can still give a weak numerical result.
algorithmExplanation = "TODO: explain one iteration and the source of numerical error" %[output:03cfd061]
%%
%[text] ## Timestep Study and Convergence Evidence
%[text] A single plausible curve is not enough. Repeat the same physical experiment at several timesteps and quantify the final-time error.
dtValues_h = [2.0 1.0 0.5 0.1];
finalEulerFraction = nan(size(dtValues_h));
relativeErrorPercent = nan(size(dtValues_h));
stepCount = nan(size(dtValues_h));
for k = 1:numel(dtValues_h)
    dtCurrent_h = dtValues_h(k);
    tCurrent_h = 0:dtCurrent_h:tMax_h;
    N_current = nan(size(tCurrent_h));
    N_current(1) = N0;
    for n = 1:numel(tCurrent_h)-1
        N_current(n+1) = N_current(n)*(1-lambda_per_h*dtCurrent_h);
    end
    finalEulerFraction(k) = N_current(end)/N0;
    relativeErrorPercent(k) = 100*abs(N_current(end)-N_exact(end))/N_exact(end);
    stepCount(k) = numel(tCurrent_h)-1;
end
convergenceRecord = table(dtValues_h(:),lambda_per_h*dtValues_h(:),stepCount(:), ... %[output:group:996407b1] %[output:2d822cab]
    finalEulerFraction(:),relativeErrorPercent(:), ... %[output:2d822cab]
    'VariableNames',{'dt_h','lambdaTimesDt','steps','EulerFinalFraction','RelativeErrorPercent'}) %[output:group:996407b1] %[output:2d822cab]
loglog(dtValues_h,relativeErrorPercent,'o-','LineWidth',1.4) %[output:05d3dde7]
grid on %[output:05d3dde7]
xlabel('Timestep, \Delta t (h)') %[output:05d3dde7]
ylabel('Final relative error (%)') %[output:05d3dde7]
title('Forward Euler convergence evidence') %[output:05d3dde7]
%[text] **Interpretation.** Describe the accuracy-cost trade-off. Do not claim convergence merely because the finest result looks smooth.
convergenceInterpretation = "TODO: use the table and plot as evidence" %[output:6495e523]
%%
%[text] ## Validation and Physical Admissibility
%[text] These executable checks test independent expectations: the initial condition, exact half-life values, finiteness, non-negativity, monotonic decay, and improvement at the smallest timestep.
assert(abs(N_exact(1)-N0) < 100*eps(max(1,N0)), ...
    'The analytical reference does not satisfy the initial condition.')
assert(abs(exp(-lambda_per_h*T_half_h)-0.5) < 1e-12, ...
    'The half-life reference check failed.')
assert(abs(N_exact(end)/N0-0.0625) < 1e-12, ...
    'The four-half-life reference check failed.')
assert(all(isfinite(N_euler)), ...
    'The numerical solution contains a non-finite value.')
assert(all(N_euler >= 0) && all(diff(N_euler) <= 0), ...
    'The numerical result violates the expected physical trend or bounds.')
assert(relativeErrorPercent(end) < relativeErrorPercent(1), ...
    'The timestep study does not show improvement at the finest resolution.')
validationStatus = "Initial-value, limiting-case, bound, trend, and convergence checks passed." %[output:422cf217]
%[text] No finite collection of checks proves a model is true. State what these checks can detect and name one model assumption they cannot validate.
validationExplanation = "TODO: explain the scope and limitation of the evidence" %[output:307570c3]
%%
%[text] ## Failure Mode: A Runnable but Unphysical Update
%[text] If $\\lambda\\Delta t\>1$, the update factor becomes negative. MATLAB can execute that formula even though a negative population is physically inadmissible.
unsafeDt_h = 10.0;
unsafeUpdateFactor = 1-lambda_per_h*unsafeDt_h;
failureRecord = table(unsafeDt_h,lambda_per_h*unsafeDt_h,unsafeUpdateFactor) %[output:63098f68]
%[text] **Diagnosis.** Predict the sign of the next value before changing the code. Propose a timestep condition that prevents this failure for the decay model.
failureExplanation = "TODO: diagnose the failure and state a defensible condition" %[output:6097a71a]
%%
